  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:D1fYPnk4VvQ",
    "purpose": "A walk through the soy sauce aisle at an Asian grocery in Seattle, explaining the main soy sauce varieties and related products and what each is used for.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Soy sauces differ a lot by country and style. Japanese, Chinese and Southeast Asian sauces each have dark, light, sweet or special versions with different uses. Knowing the terms, such as light meaning color versus low sodium, helps you pick the right bottle.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Japanese koikuchi (dark) soy sauce is the everyday shoyu. Usukuchi (light) is lighter in color but saltier, is made with more wheat, and is used more in Kansai cuisine.",
      "Amakuchi is a sweetened usukuchi. White soy sauce uses even more wheat, has a shorter fermentation and a more delicate flavor.",
      "Liquid aminos are not soy sauce and are not really interchangeable with it.",
      "In recipes, 'light soy sauce' may mean low sodium soy sauce rather than usukuchi; the two should not be confused.",
      "Tamari is made mostly or entirely from soybeans and can replace koikuchi for gluten-intolerant cooks, but check the label for gluten-free because some tamari contains wheat.",
      "Chinese dark soy sauce is thick, sweet and used mainly for color. Chinese light soy sauce is the main seasoning soy sauce, and stir fries often combine the two.",
      "Japanese koikuchi sits between Chinese light and dark soy sauce, so it is the one bottle to keep if you only keep one.",
      "Filipino soy sauce is sweeter, darker and saltier. Indonesian sweet soy sauce is a thick syrup used for dishes like Indonesian fried rice."
    ],
    "outline": [
